// The cases of sycl-accessor-buffer-identity.cir, run through both the
// legacy KernelBindingTable trace (LEGACY, the same verdicts as there) and
// the points-to SYCL models (PT): same `$storage` ghost node (or host
// memory, or pointer target) = the same memory; distinct and complete =
// distinct. The inputs are that test's, plus the roles CIRGen gives the
// launch glue, the USM allocation and getDeviceKernelInfo (untagged there,
// so the legacy output does not change), and a body for std::vector::data,
// which points-to analyses rather than models. The first eleven cases are
// identity cases: ten agree, one is a context-insensitivity mismatch
// (DISTINCT HOST MEMORY). The last four are policy cases (local accessor,
// nested accessor, pointer in a struct, two launches): they agree too.

// -----
// DISTINCT: three local buffers, each accessed once.
// buffer(range) buffers own fresh storage, so the three accessors are pairwise
// no-alias; the accessors are built both ways (constructor and get_access).

